<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marquis de Lafayette</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a>–<a href="/years/1834">1834</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lafayette, La Fayette, Gilbert du Motier, Hero of the Two Worlds</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> du Motier, Marquis de La Fayette, was a French military officer and politician who volunteered to serve in the Continental Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, commanding troops at the siege of Yorktown in 1781. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he helped write the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a> and commanded the National Guard after the storming of the Bastille, but fled in 1792 and spent more than five years in Austrian prisons. He later sat as a liberal in the Chamber of Deputies, toured all 24 American states in 1824 and, in the July Revolution of 1830, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis-Philippe</a> as king. He is known as the Hero of the Two Worlds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marquis-de-lafayette.jpg" alt="Marquis de Lafayette" width="330" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph-Désiré Court · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilbert_du_Motier_Marquis_de_Lafayette.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
